A||||11||JohnnyK||What is the right way to bleed the clutch.||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-22-2000||04:48 PM||||The fluid in teh master is down to maybe 1" to .5" (is this low enough to let air in the system) and the clutch doesn't really disengage. I've searched through old threads, but can't really get an answer. Hayes says to open the bleeder, press the clutch, and then close the bleeder while the clutch is in. But with brakes, you pump it, hold it down, and THEN close the bleeder valve? Shouldn't you do this with the clutch to. Also, I read someones post where you can just open the bleeder screw, and let it drip out while topping off the reservoir every so often. Do i have to raise the wheels for this? Does it work? Thanks (sorry about the long post)||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||08-22-2000||06:08 PM||||Ok, there was NO fluid in the reservoir, so I bled the system (until I saw new fluid coming out, maybe 15-20 strokes, do I need more) because I was having a HORRIBLe time shifting. Real hard to go into 1 or 2. But it made no difference. I saw no air bubbles come out either. Make any sense? (some small bubbles came out, but I thought there would be a huge one from teh reservoir being empty)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick||08-22-2000||08:13 PM||||<BR>I don't want to ruin your day, but if you had NO fluid in the reservoir, you've got problems somewhere. You're going to have to find where the leak is and fix it. Keep in mind that anywhere that fluid leaks out is also a location where air can be sucked in. For what it's worth, you have my sympathy. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000003||JohnnyK||08-22-2000||08:30 PM||||That was my guess, only I can't find any place where it is leaking. There has never been a wet spot on the ground. Anyone else had this problem? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||08-22-2000||08:40 PM||||<BR>It's doubtful that the leaking fluid would ever make it to the ground in this situation (unless a hydraulic line suddenly burst). You'll have to follow the line from the master to the slave cylinder and see if there's any moisture and/or dirt stuck to moisture evident. Obviously check around both the master and slave seals for the same thing. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000005||webbee||08-22-2000||09:02 PM||||Look in the forum archives. There is a ton of material on this subject.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||08-22-2000||10:48 PM||||I'll give you my $.02.  <BR>First off, I use a Mity-Vac for brake and clutch hydraulics.  It works great, and it is well worth the $25 I paid for it at Wally World.  After helping my dad bleed Vette brakes without one as a kid, I knew I had to have one. <BR>My clutch master cylinder started to leak at the front bulkhead 2 years ago.  The floor mat had a little fluid on it, and the pedal got real ineffective after a long trip (30+ miles).  I put a rebuild kit into it, with no problems so far.<BR>When I finished my clutch job last week, the pedal was really mushy, so I tried bleeding the system (same thing happened last clutch job, and it worked then).  My bleeder screw was really rusty, and it was letting air get sucked past the threads.  You cannot get a new bleeder screw without a rebuild kit at a minimum.  Put on a new slave cylinder, and bled the system.  Problem solved.  The Mity-Vac also works great to flush all of the old, nasty, moisture containing fluid from your lines, too.  <BR>mrfiero has posted about the problems he has had with the aftermarket (cast iron) slave cylinders, so beware.  I couldn't wait around for my dealer to get me a GM piece at 3 times the cost Discount Auto wanted for one (overnight).||||reg||1
Z||000007||mshill||08-23-2000||03:01 AM||||Johnny,  I had clutch problems for some time and then after fixing my hydrolics I found (when I took the engine out for the rebuild) that the clutch disc was worn out.  After replacing it I had to bleed the entire system one more time.  I found as many others have that because of the location of the bleeder screw on the slave, you cannot get all the air out of the system without pushing the slave plunger back into the slave.  Do this about four times (closing the bleeder before  you let it come back) and you will have a solid clutch.  Now my wife drives the car whenever she gets a chance cause it shifts like new.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||08-23-2000||08:17 AM||||Jacking up the left rear side of thecar helps get the air bubbles to rise to the bleeder side of the slave cylinder.<P>I agree with MSHILL that since the bleeder screw is on the opposite side of the slave cylinder from the plunger you need to compress the plunger while the bleeder is open to expel the bubbles trapped at the other end and then close the bleeder screw before you let the plunger back out.<P>My master cylinder went out and I rebuilt it.  I mentioned this to Ed Parks and he said to expect the slave to go at any time.  Sure enough, the next week the slave failed.  I could not find a rebuild kit so I got a rebuilt unit.  So far so good after 18 months.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||08-23-2000||08:19 AM||||BTW,<P>I also use a Mity-Vac and it works great.  I got it on the clearance rack at WalMart for $12.00.  I think the regular price is around $25 or $30.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000010||Alex4mula||08-23-2000||08:59 AM||||I also agree that the Miti-Vac is super. Last time I wanted to rebleed I screwed up and spent two days trying to bleed it with the normal method. The only way it worked was to suck & suck & suck with the Miti until my hand ached and closed the bleeder screw. Didn't even had to pump it. Clutch was better than ever.||||reg||1
Z||000011||JohnnyK||08-23-2000||03:21 PM||||How can I tell in specific if a master or slave cylinder has gone out? They appear to work (don't know what that means) Well, i just checked the reservoir, and it is still full, and I took it on a fairly long drive yesterday (50-60 miles). So does this mean it is NOT leaking? Where else would my problem lie. And why would there have been no fluid in the reservoir before?||||reg||1
